<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thyra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Queen Thyra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thyra was the wife of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorm-the-old">Gorm the Old</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a>, who lived in the tenth century. Gorm raised the smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelling">Jelling</a> runestone in her memory, calling her &quot;Denmark's adornment&quot;, one of the earliest inscriptions to name Denmark. Later legend credited her with building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danevirke">Danevirke</a> against the Germans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thyra.jpg" alt="Thyra" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographer Roberto Fortuna, commisioned by the Danish National Museum · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jellingsten_lille_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
